WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Speaker 1: So we just had another election here in&nbsp;
the United States a couple of weeks ago. And one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.080
would imagine that around now would be the time&nbsp;
for Republicans to start unveiling some specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:17.700
examples of voter fraud that supposedly happened&nbsp;
across the U.S. in these 2022 midterms. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.820
because they've been focusing on it nonstop as&nbsp;
an issue for their party. They've also started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:31.380
a bunch of state level law enforcement units that&nbsp;
were set up to investigate specific voter fraud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.140
instances. And so one would think that with this&nbsp;
being their first opportunity to unveil the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:42.660
about double voting by Democrats and Democrats&nbsp;
voting for their dead grandma and people putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.860
the wrong kind of sauce on the ballot and all&nbsp;
these different things, you'd think that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.320
would be talking about this stuff nonstop, but&nbsp;
actually they've been very much hush hush. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.200
that's because in Florida, Georgia and Virginia,&nbsp;
where these election fraud agencies were created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.180
all backed by Republicans, they just don't&nbsp;
have the examples to put forward to people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.340
And it shouldn't be much of a surprise that&nbsp;
they're coming up short, because we know and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.120
we've known this whole time that voter fraud&nbsp;
is incredibly rare in the United States. Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.340
study that we have on this, the Brennan Center of&nbsp;
Justice Study, for example, show that it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:23.760
a handful of cases out of billions of votes cast&nbsp;
across many, many different election cycles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.640
you have someone who's provably committed fraud.&nbsp;
And many of the examples of voter fraud from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.140
2020 election were actually Republicans who were&nbsp;
voting for Trump, thinking that they were making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.700
up the difference because the left was supposedly&nbsp;
committing so much fraud. Surprise, surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.760
they weren't. But the Trump people in some cases&nbsp;
were. And of course, voting twice. Voter fraud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.280
in general is just rare because it's the type&nbsp;
of thing that could get you sent to prison and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.120
you're risking potential jail time for one vote.&nbsp;
That's probably not going to make the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.500
in an election. It's probably going to end up&nbsp;
being inconsequential. So it just doesn't seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.740
like a fair trade for the average person to want&nbsp;
to make. That's why when DeSantis wanted to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:14.820
his voter fraud stunt earlier this year and find&nbsp;
people to charge an arrest for for voter fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.180
he had to choose people who thought that&nbsp;
they actually were able to legally vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.620
thought that they had their rights restored from&nbsp;
the ballot measure in Florida back in 2018 on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.620
restoring felon voter rights. And it's only these&nbsp;
fringe cases that you can really find to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:35.160
make your case for how this is supposedly existing&nbsp;
all across the country. There's just not that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.680
cases of people willingly doing this. And so a&nbsp;
special task force in all of these states never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.080
really made sense. But of course, it was just&nbsp;
to appeal to right wing voters. But even so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.800
they're not succeeding at doing that because&nbsp;
they're not able to come up with at least just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.660
a couple of cases to parade out to people and say&nbsp;
that this is systemic across the entire country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.740
It's only been a couple weeks, so maybe we'll&nbsp;
just have to give them more time. But sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.480
Republican voters will just look at this all. And&nbsp;
even if there's a lack of evidence, they'll say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.040
oh, see, that means that Democrats have gotten&nbsp;
especially good at rigging the elections because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.960
they can cover their tracks so easily. And it&nbsp;
means that we have to pour even more resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.540
into trying to catch them. I will say, though,&nbsp;
that the voter fraud thing does seem like it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:25.500
calming down after this 2022 midterm cycle. The&nbsp;
only person that's really trying to make it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.840
focal point is Carrie Lake, who lost her election&nbsp;
in Arizona. But these other Trumpian candidates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.900
like Doug Mastriano and Tudor Dickson in Michigan,&nbsp;
they've just decided to concede. So at least for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:42.600
now, the voter fraud lies are taking a break. But&nbsp;
I do think that they'll be back in 2024 if Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:48.360
decides to run and if he ultimately ends up losing&nbsp;
either the primary or the general. So they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.040
taking a break from this narrative now, but you&nbsp;
better believe it's probably going to come back.

